-- title:
--   Evaluated Slice Phase Six First Reverse Bound
-- statement:
--   **The first reverse insertion costs at most $2\sqrt{\zeta}$.**
--
--   Let $S$ be a symmetric strategy for parameters $(q,m,d)$ with normalized state, let `family` be a slice-indexed polynomial submeasurement family, and let $\zeta$ be an error scale. Assume that, under the uniform distribution on evaluated-slice questions, the state-dependent squared-distance relation between the left evaluated point family at the first point $Q_1$ and the corresponding total-sandwich family holds with bound $4\zeta$. If `evaluatedSlicePhaseFivePaperRemoved` and `evaluatedSlicePhaseSixFirstRemoved` denote the phase-five and phase-six scalar endpoints, then
--
--   $$
--   \left|
--   \mathbb{E}_Q[\mathrm{evaluatedSlicePhaseFivePaperRemoved}(Q)]
--   -\mathbb{E}_Q[\mathrm{evaluatedSlicePhaseSixFirstRemoved}(Q)]
--   \right|
--   \le 2\sqrt{\zeta}.
--   $$
--
--   This is the first reverse insertion after the phase-five removal, corresponding to paper lines 99--102. It supplies the first half of the combined reverse-insertion estimate used by the scalar commutativity chain.
--
--   Formalization credit: Sirui Lu, Ruixuan Deng, Yanqiao Zhu, and Zhengfeng Ji (arXiv:2609.19814). Mathematical source: Zhengfeng Ji, Anand Natarajan, Thomas Vidick, John Wright, and Henry Yuen (arXiv:2009.12982).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The source declaration is `MIPStarRE.LDT.Commutativity.evaluatedSlice_phaseSix_first_reverse_bound`; its Lean statement gives the exact quantifier order and all boundary hypotheses.
